{#- RB4011 — HD-1097 DHCP option 15 (search suffix = {{ domain_local }}) DELTA, 2026-10-07.
    Why: `\\nas\media` is typed with a BARE host name. The LAN zone answered service names
    only (nas.kogler.si → NXDOMAIN, aa flag, on both home resolvers), so the mount rode the
    LLMNR/NetBIOS broadcast fallback — which a wired switch port supplies and a Wi-Fi client
    does not. Measured on the owner's laptop: mounting by IP over Wi-Fi works, by name it does
    not. Same change set: the host A records in `zone_kogler_si` (group_vars/all) answer the
    FQDN; THIS delta delivers option 15 so the bare name is devolved to the FQDN in the first
    place. One without the other leaves the bug half-fixed.

    ⚠ Mechanism: `dhcp-option`, NOT `domain=`. `/ip dhcp-server set … domain=kogler.si` →
    `Script Error: bad parameter domain` on 7.24.4. v6 had that property; v7 has none on either
    `dhcp-server` or `dhcp-server network` (the API print shows `dhcp-option: ""`, no `domain`).
    So: one option row, code 15, attached to every server. docs/network-vlans.md §DHCP owns the why.

    ⚠⚠ Two syntax traps, both paid for on the live device with this delta:
      1. The VALUE is hex with a `0x` prefix. `value=kogler.si`, the quoted form and `value=x6b…`
         all die with `failure: Unknown data type!`; `value=1` dies with `Bad delimiter!`.
         `raw-value` (in `print detail`) is the field to read back.
      2. The hex literal must appear IN THE COMMAND. `:local dom "0x6b6f…"` + `set … value=$dom`
         reports success and writes an EMPTY value — the variable form is not hex-parsed. The
         first import of this delta did exactly that and silently blanked the option it had just
         created; only `print detail` showed it. Keep the literal inline, never a `$var`.

    Folded back into rb4011_converge.rsc.j2 in the SAME change (roles/router deliberately does
    not manage this reference) — a delta is transient and must never become a second truth
    (docs/network-ops.md §The three tiers). Idempotent: guarded add, value re-asserted from the
    literal, and attaching the same reference again is a no-op. Reaches clients at their next
    ACK/renewal; nothing else about DHCP behaviour changes. -#}
# =============================================================================
# HD-1097 — DHCP option 15 on EVERY dhcp-server, dhcp-mgmt included
# =============================================================================
:local optName "dhcp-domain"

:if ([/ip dhcp-server option find where name=$optName] = "") do={
    /ip dhcp-server option add name=$optName code=15 value=0x{{ (domain_local | string).encode().hex() }}
} else={
    /ip dhcp-server option set [/ip dhcp-server option find where name=$optName] code=15 value=0x{{ (domain_local | string).encode().hex() }}
}
:put ("HD-1097: option " . $optName . " code=15 raw-value=" . [/ip dhcp-server option get [/ip dhcp-server option find where name=$optName] raw-value])

:foreach s in=[/ip dhcp-server find] do={
    :local n [/ip dhcp-server get $s name]
    /ip dhcp-server set $s dhcp-option=[/ip dhcp-server option find where name=$optName]
    :put ("HD-1097: " . $n . " dhcp-option -> " . [/ip dhcp-server get $s dhcp-option])
}

# The `:foreach` + `get` form is deliberate: over non-interactive SSH a filtered `print where …`
# and `print as-value` print NOTHING and a bare `[find]` resolves against the CURRENT menu, so
# both read as "not set" while the value IS set (measured 2026-10-07, HD-1095). Every `[find]`
# here therefore carries its full path. But trust `get … raw-value` over `get … value` — the
# latter can read empty on a row that is fine, so the honest single-line read is:
#     /ip dhcp-server option print detail        → value="0x…" raw-value="6b6f…"
#
# Verify (device):  /ip dhcp-server option print detail
#                   /ip dhcp-server print detail  → every row carries dhcp-option
# Verify (client):  Windows: ipconfig /renew, then ipconfig /all →
#                   "Connection-specific DNS Suffix . : kogler.si"
#                   then: Resolve-DnsName nas.kogler.si → the nas Home address (SSOT row `nas`)
